<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Courland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kurzeme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Courland is a historical region of western Latvia, lying between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ventspils">Ventspils</a>. Conquered by the Livonian Order in the thirteenth century, it became the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia under Polish-Lithuanian suzerainty in 1561 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1795. Predominantly Lutheran, it has formed part of Latvia since the country's independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/courland.jpg" alt="Courland" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgars Šulcs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ventspils_viduslaiku_pils_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Latvian national consciousness had grown since the national awakening of the mid-nineteenth century, and in 1915 Latvian Riflemen units were formed in the Russian army to defend <b>Courland</b> and Riga against the Germans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Courland</b> was conquered by the Livonian Order in the 1250s and 1260s, the bishop received about a third of the land, scattered in several districts.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-courland">Bishopric of Courland</a></li><li><blockquote>It depended on supplies from <b>Courland</b> and on agreements with the kings of Kombo and Niumi.</blockquote><a href="/state/courland-gambia">Courland Gambia</a></li><li><blockquote>They landed at what became Great Courland Bay, built Fort Jacobus and the settlement of Jacobusstadt, and named the island New <b>Courland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/courland-tobago">Courland Tobago</a></li><li><blockquote>The dukes of <b>Courland</b> repeatedly claimed the district and controlled it for periods, while the nobles resisted, preferring the distant Polish king.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-pilten">District of Pilten</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Livonian Order dissolved in 1561, its last master, Gotthard Kettler, secularised the Order's lands south of the Daugava and became hereditary Duke of <b>Courland</b> and Semigallia, a vassal of Poland-Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a></li></ul></section>
